Xenith — Notice of Provenance
==============================

Xenith is licensed GPL-3.0 (see LICENSE). This file names what Xenith's
implementation specifically inherits from earlier plugins in the same
Elo/head-to-head ranking lineage for Stash, beyond the general acknowledgment
in README.md.

Ascension (GPL-3.0)
--------------------
https://github.com/Servbot91/Sakotos-Stash-Repo/tree/main/plugins/Ascension

The following are carried over from Ascension, with Xenith's own constants
or mechanism changes noted where they apply:

- The six-tier S/A/B/C/D/F rating tier system, including the letter set,
  tier count, and ordering. Xenith's tier *bounds* are independently
  calibrated via Monte Carlo simulation against Xenith's own rating math
  (see `src/elo.js`'s `TIER_BOUNDS` and `qa/scripts/simulate-tier-bounds.mjs`);
  the tier frame itself is Ascension's.
- The session repeat-opponent penalty mechanism and its three weight bands
  (`src/matchmaking.js`).
- The sigmoid form of the K-factor experience decay, including its midpoint
  and slope (`src/elo.js`'s `experienceFactor`); Xenith changes the
  asymptote and makes the endpoints library-scaled rather than fixed.
- The low-match boost mechanism and its three multiplier constants
  (`src/matchmaking.js`); Xenith changes the baseline calculation.
- The forced cross-tier match event, its trigger probability, and its
  minimum rating-gap threshold (`src/matchmaking.js`).
- The top-N weighted seed pool concept and its pool size
  (`src/matchmaking.js`).
- The recently-selected candidate tracking mechanism
  (`addToRecentlySelected`/`isRecentlySelected` in `src/matchmaking.js`);
  Xenith changes the buffer size and namespaces it per battle type.
- The opponent-selection failover ordering (anchor window, then a wider
  window, then nearest-rating, then random) and the two-candidate minimum
  guard (`src/matchmaking.js`).
- The rating-gap threshold at which upset attenuation begins to apply
  (`src/elo.js`). The attenuation curve itself past that threshold is
  Xenith's own — see the HotOrNot lineage and independent-work notes below.
- Eleven CSS class names in Xenith's sidebar and modal-action markup
  (`src/styles/xenith.css`, `src/components/`), listed for completeness:
  `hon-plugin-layout`, `hon-main-plugin-content`, `hon-sidebar`,
  `hon-sidebar-row`, `hon-sidebar-row-text`, `hon-sidebar-subrow`,
  `hon-sidebar-expandable`, `hon-sidebar-expanded-content`,
  `hon-sidebar-expand-icon`, `hon-action-btn`,
  `hon-battle-rank-badge-compact`. (As of this notice these are being
  renamed to a `xen-` prefix; see CHANGELOG for the version this lands in.)

HotOrNot / HotOrNotV2 / HotOrNot_V3 / Stash Battle (AGPL-3.0)
---------------------------------------------------------------
https://github.com/lowgrade12/hot-or-not
https://github.com/Lurking987/stash-plugins/tree/main/plugins/hot_or_not
https://github.com/dtt-git/stash-battle

The following predate Ascension and are inherited from this earlier
lineage, which Ascension itself also draws on:

- The `custom_fields` persisted-stats schema (`total_matches`, `wins`,
  `losses`, `draws`, `current_streak`, `best_streak`, `worst_streak`,
  `last_match`) and the legacy key names Xenith reads as a migration
  fallback (`hotornot_stats`, `performer_record` — see `src/matchmaking.js`
  and `backend/tasks.py`'s `task_migrate`).
- The three-match-mode structure (a Swiss-style pairwise mode, a
  ladder-placement mode, and an incumbent-defense mode). Xenith's
  implementations of each — the Bayesian posterior placement in
  `src/gauntlet.js` and the reign reducer in `src/champion.js` — are its
  own; the three-mode structure itself is not.
- The 15-point rating-gap opponent anchor window (`src/matchmaking.js`).
- Neutral default initialization of an unrated item at the midpoint of the
  rating scale (`DEFAULT_RATING` in `src/elo.js`).
- The general approach of compressing the logistic expected-score divisor
  below standard Elo's D=400 (Xenith's specific D=35 value is its own; see
  CHANGELOG's Rating model section).
- Roughly 29 further shared CSS class names beyond the eleven listed above
  under Ascension, present throughout this earlier lineage as well
  (`hon-modal*`, `hon-vs-*`, `hon-stats-*`, and others in
  `src/styles/xenith.css`).

Independent work
-----------------
Xenith's entropy-weighted candidate pairing priority (`src/elo.js`'s
`entropy`/`priorityScore`, and their use throughout `src/matchmaking.js`),
its library-scaled K-factor endpoint calculation
(`computeSystemConfig` in `src/elo.js`), its single symmetric upset
attenuation formula, its uncertainty-discounted composite score, its
Monte-Carlo-calibrated tier bounds, its match-count-based cooldown buffers,
and its Bayesian gauntlet placement engine (`src/gauntlet.js`) have no
counterpart in the plugins named above and were developed independently for
Xenith.
